": "/h1/opencode-src", "other_copies": [], "output_tail": "[tool: bash]\n[tool: write]\n[tool: bash]\nI wrote the polyglot to `polyglot/main.rs`. It compiled with both `rustc` and `g++ -x c++`. For N = 0, 1, 2, 3, 10, 50, 90 and 150, both programs printed the same answers, starting 1, 1, 2, 3 as you specified.\n\n**How it works:**\n- In C++, a `//` comment ending in `\\` carries on to the next line. In Rust it doesn't. So the `/*` on line 2 is just part of a comment for C++, but for Rust it starts a block comment that hides the whole C++ section.\n- Inside the C++ part, `#if 0` makes the C++ compiler skip the Rust code. A later `//\\` line followed by `*/` closes Rust's block comment, so Rust compiles that code.\n- At the end, the same trick hides the closing `#endif` from Rust.\n\n**Limit:** both versions use 128-bit unsigned integers (`u128` in Rust, `unsigned __int128` in C++), so results are correct up to N = 184. Above that, the Rust build (no optimisation by default) stops with an overflow error, and the C++ build silently prints a wrapped-around wrong number.\n\nI deleted the compiled test binaries afterwards, so only `main.rs` is left in the folder.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [], "params_stripped_requests": 0, "permission_rejections": 0, "pin_source": "override", "pin_source_class": "override", "probe_method": "manifest", "protocol_model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider_loaded_mismatch": null, "provider_loaded_source": "none", "provider_loaded_version": null, "provider_manifest_version": "2.0.0", "provider_pin_sha256": "1c264120eff5e204a01446456369ad1fe7eb19799f13d5dd0f90f98b1b692e91", "provider_trace": "off", "provider_version_proven": false, "provider_version_source": "manifest", "provider_versions": {"@ai-sdk/anthropic": "2.0.0"}, "proxy_cost_usd": 0.2008084, "proxy_observed": [{"reasoning_tokens": null, "thinking_mode": "default", "thinking_sent": "none"}, {"reasoning_tokens": null, "thinking_mode": "default", "thinking_sent": "none"}, {"rea